Actually, I like trying the protection racket with a flame mage. That
hell hound is a tank even as a cub, and can successfully kill the
mine town priest as an adult (at least with a bit of luck, perhaps a
wand of speed or a wand of healing). So you have good protection on
the way down, some spellbooks to sell for money, a /oFire to engrave
Elbereth, and a nearly guaranteed altar after buying protection.

Play a gnome, level up quickly afterwards to level 7, and 'tinker' one
of the guards' whistles up to a magic whistle. Tinkering takes a lot
of time (I usually settle down in a corner of Sokoban with plenty of
food nearby), but can get you some very nice stuff, including
converting boring but enchanted dwarven cloaks into handy and still
enchanted CoMagRes. Gnomes also get a lot of undead in the mines; the
gnomish mummies are dangerous (Elbereth works well), but the zombies
provide a useful bit of alignment boost at not many xp. And the hell
hound won't stop to eat all the time if there are fewer edible
corpses... The down side is that gnomes can't two-weapon which may be
a liability later in the game.

